"""Lecture notes on three distinct topics, long enough to chunk and cluster."""

PHOTOSYNTHESIS = """Photosynthesis

Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose. It takes place in the chloroplasts, which contain the green pigment chlorophyll. Chlorophyll absorbs mostly red and blue light and reflects green light, which is why leaves look green. The overall reaction uses carbon dioxide and water and releases oxygen as a by-product.

The light-dependent reactions happen in the thylakoid membranes of the chloroplast. Light energy splits water molecules, releasing oxygen, and the energy is captured as ATP and NADPH. These energy carriers move into the stroma, the fluid that surrounds the thylakoids inside the chloroplast.

The Calvin cycle is the set of light-independent reactions that happen in the stroma. The enzyme rubisco fixes carbon dioxide onto a five-carbon sugar. Using the ATP and NADPH made by the light reactions, the cycle produces a three-carbon sugar that the plant uses to build glucose and starch. The rate of photosynthesis depends on light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature."""

WORLD_WAR = """The First World War

The First World War began in 1914 after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo. A system of alliances quickly drew the great powers of Europe into the conflict. Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire formed the Central Powers, while France, Russia and Britain led the Allies.

Trench warfare is a type of fighting where opposing armies dig long defensive trenches. On the Western Front, soldiers faced machine guns, barbed wire and artillery bombardments across no man's land. Battles such as the Somme and Verdun caused enormous casualties while moving the front line only a few miles.

The war ended with an armistice on 11 November 1918. The Treaty of Versailles, signed in 1919, forced Germany to accept responsibility for the war, give up territory and pay heavy reparations. Many historians argue that the harsh terms of the treaty created resentment in Germany that contributed to the Second World War."""

PLATE_TECTONICS = """Plate Tectonics

Plate tectonics is the theory that the outer shell of the Earth is divided into large rigid plates that move slowly over the mantle. The lithosphere is the rigid outer layer made of the crust and the uppermost mantle. Convection currents in the hot mantle beneath are thought to drive the movement of the plates.

At divergent boundaries plates move apart and new crust forms as magma rises, for example along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. At convergent boundaries plates collide; when an oceanic plate meets a continental plate, the denser oceanic plate sinks beneath it in a process called subduction, which forms deep trenches and volcanoes.

At transform boundaries plates slide past each other horizontally. The San Andreas Fault in California is a well-known transform boundary. Stress builds up where the plates lock together and is released suddenly as earthquakes. Earthquakes and volcanoes are therefore concentrated along plate boundaries."""

NOTES = "\n\n".join([PHOTOSYNTHESIS, WORLD_WAR, PLATE_TECTONICS])
